[section:quickstart Quick Start]

This chapter walks through what you need to build against Boost.Taar,
how to bring it into your own project, the smallest useful HTTP server
you can write with it, and a tour of the four idioms you will see again
and again:

* [link boost_taar.quickstart.requirements Requirements]: supported
  compilers, Boost components, and CMake version.
* [link boost_taar.quickstart.installation Installation]: header-only
  drop-in, Boost super-project, and CMake/Conan integration.
* [link boost_taar.quickstart.hello A Hello World HTTP Server]: a
  complete program serving a single GET endpoint.
* [link boost_taar.quickstart.tour A Whirlwind Tour]: stock responses,
  JSON, path arguments, static files, hand-written handlers, per-connection
  and per-session state, JSON-RPC, MCP, OpenAPI, and graceful shutdown.

[section:requirements Requirements]

* A C++23 conforming compiler. Boost.Taar is exercised on recent GCC, Clang
  and MSVC toolchains.
* Boost 1.84 or later, with the __Asio__, __Beast__, __Url__, __Json__, and
  __System__ libraries available.
* CMake 3.28 or later if you build against the bundled `CMakeLists.txt`.

[endsect]

[section:installation Installation]

Taar is header-only. Drop the `include/` directory on your include path and
link against the compiled Boost libraries that __Asio__, __Beast__,
__Url__, __Json__, and __System__ already require for your application.

Inside a Boost super-project the library participates in the modular build
through `build.jam`; no extra configuration is required.

[endsect]

[section:hello A Hello World HTTP Server]

The smallest useful program registers a single REST endpoint and runs an
acceptor on port 8080:

    #include <boost/taar/handler/rest.hpp>
    #include <boost/taar/matcher/method.hpp>
    #include <boost/taar/matcher/target.hpp>
    #include <boost/taar/server/tcp.hpp>
    #include <boost/taar/session/http.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/co_spawn.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/detached.hpp>
    #include <boost/asio/io_context.hpp>
    #include <boost/beast/http/verb.hpp>

    int main()
    {
        namespace taar = boost::taar;
        namespace http = boost::beast::http;
        using taar::matcher::method;
        using taar::matcher::target;

        boost::asio::io_context io;

        taar::session::http http_session;
        http_session.register_request_handler(
            method == http::verb::get && target == "/hello",
            taar::handler::rest([]{ return "Hello, world!"; }));

        taar::cancellation_signals signals;
        boost::asio::co_spawn(
            io,
            taar::server::tcp("0.0.0.0", "8080", http_session, signals),
            boost::asio::detached);

        io.run();
    }

For a full version that includes signal handling and graceful shutdown,
see the [link boost_taar.examples examples chapter].

[endsect]

[section:tour A Whirlwind Tour]

[heading Stock response]

    taar::session::http http_session;
    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/version",
        taar::handler::rest([]{ return "1.0"; }));

[heading JSON response with path arguments]

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}",
        taar::handler::rest(
            [](int a, int b)
            {
                return boost::json::value {
                    {"a", a},
                    {"b", b},
                    {"result", a + b}
                };
            },
            taar::handler::path_arg("a"),
            taar::handler::path_arg("b")));

[heading Asynchronous handler]

A handler may be a coroutine. Declare its result type as
`taar::awaitable<T>` and `co_await` other asynchronous work (here a
lookup that hits the network or a database) before producing the
response. The session awaits the handler, so the connection is never
blocked while the work is in flight:

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/user/{id}",
        taar::handler::rest(
            [](int id) -> taar::awaitable<boost::json::value>
            {
                auto user = co_await find_user(id);  // co_awaits I/O
                co_return boost::json::value {
                    {"id", id},
                    {"name", user.name}
                };
            },
            taar::handler::path_arg("id")));

The argument extraction and the `response_from` conversion of the
returned value work exactly as in the synchronous case; only the result
type and the `co_return` change.

[heading Serving static files]

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/{*path}",
        taar::handler::htdocs {document_root});

[heading Custom handler]

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::put && target == "/special/{*path}",
        [](http::request<http::empty_body> const& request,
           taar::matcher::context const& context)
            -> http::message_generator
        {
            http::response<http::string_body> res {
                http::status::ok, request.version()};
            res.set(http::field::content_type, "text/plain");
            res.keep_alive(request.keep_alive());
            res.body() = "Special path is: " + context.path_args.at("path");
            res.prepare_payload();
            return res;
        });

[heading Per-connection state]

Give the session a context type and ask for it in a handler. One object
is created per TCP connection and shared by every request on it:

    struct conn_state { int requests = 0; };

    taar::session::basic_http<conn_state> http_session;
    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/stats",
        taar::handler::rest([](conn_state& c)
        {
            return boost::json::value {{"requests", ++c.requests}};
        }));

[heading Cookie sessions]

Keyed state lives in a `context_store`; `with_session` resolves the key
from a cookie (or header), creates the entry when allowed, locks it for
the request and injects it:

    struct user_session { int visits = 0; };
    taar::context_store<std::string, user_session> sessions {std::chrono::minutes {30}};

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/",
        taar::handler::with_session(
            sessions,
            taar::handler::session_key::cookie {"sid"},
            taar::handler::session_policy::create_if_missing,
            taar::handler::rest([](user_session& s) { return ++s.visits; })));

With `session_policy::require` a request without a valid key is answered
`401` before the handler runs.

[heading JSON-RPC 2.0]

An endpoint owns a registry of methods and hands you one handler for one
route. Batches, notifications and error codes are handled for you:

    taar::jsonrpc::endpoint<> rpc;
    rpc.method("add", [](int a, int b) { return a + b; },
        taar::jsonrpc::rpc_arg("a"), taar::jsonrpc::rpc_arg("b"));

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::post && target == "/rpc", rpc.handler());

[heading MCP]

An MCP endpoint composes a JSON-RPC endpoint and adds the protocol's
lifecycle, tools, resources and sessions. The tool's `inputSchema` is
derived from the callable's parameter types:

    struct notes { std::vector<std::string> items; };
    taar::mcp::endpoint<notes> mcp {{.name = "notes", .version = "1.0.0"}};
    mcp.tool("notes/add", "Append a note",
        [](notes& n, std::string text) { n.items.push_back(text); },
        taar::mcp::mcp_arg("text", "The note"));

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/mcp", mcp.stream_handler());
    http_session.register_request_handler(target == "/mcp", mcp.handler());

[heading OpenAPI]

Describe routes as you register them and serve the generated document:

    taar::openapi::generator openapi {{.title = "Demo", .version = "1.0.0"}};

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/api/sum/{a}/{b}",
        taar::handler::rest([](int a, int b) { return a + b; },
            taar::handler::path_arg("a", "First addend"),
            taar::handler::path_arg("b", "Second addend")),
        openapi.describe("Add two numbers"));

    http_session.register_request_handler(
        method == http::verb::get && target == "/openapi.json", openapi.handler());

[heading Graceful shutdown]

Emit the cancellation signals from a signal handler instead of stopping
the `io_context`: the acceptor stops, in-flight requests complete, idle
connections close, and `io.run()` returns:

    taar::cancellation_signals signals;
    boost::asio::signal_set os_signals {io, SIGINT, SIGTERM};
    os_signals.async_wait([&](auto, auto) { signals.emit(); });

    boost::asio::co_spawn(
        io,
        taar::server::tcp("0.0.0.0", "8080", http_session, signals),
        boost::asio::bind_cancellation_slot(signals.slot(), taar::ignore_and_rethrow));

[endsect]

[endsect]
